<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heidelberg Catechism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1563">1563</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> Catechism is a Reformed Protestant statement of faith in question-and-answer form, published in 1563 at the order of Elector Frederick III of the Palatinate. Written chiefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus">Zacharias Ursinus</a> with Caspar Olevian, it made the Palatinate the leading Calvinist state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It became one of the most widely used confessions of Reformed churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and beyond.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism.jpg" alt="Heidelberg Catechism" width="320" height="504" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heidelberger_Katechismus_1563_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Frederick III, who ruled the Electoral Palatinate from 1559 to 1576, was the first German prince to profess Reformed doctrine while officially Lutheran. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555 had granted toleration only to Lutherans under Lutheran princes, and Frederick sought to even out the religious situation of his largely Lutheran realm within the mainly Roman Catholic Holy Roman Empire. He commissioned a catechism meant to give a fixed form of Christian doctrine, to instruct the young in school and church and to equip pastors and teachers. It was also aimed against Roman Catholic teaching, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> and strict Gnesio-Lutherans such as Tilemann Heshusius and Matthias Flacius, who resisted his Reformed leanings, above all on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The introduction credits the whole theological faculty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and the superintendents of the church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus">Zacharias Ursinus</a> is usually regarded as the principal author, and Frederick wrote the preface and closely oversaw the work. Scholars disagree on its theological roots, seeing it as Genevan, as Zurich Reformed in the spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a>, or as both Reformed and Lutheran, especially Melanchthonian.</p><p>The text has 129 questions grouped into 52 Lord's Days, one for each Sunday of the year. After two opening questions it has three parts: the misery of man, man's redemption, which expounds the Apostles' Creed and the sacraments, and thankfulness, which treats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-prayer">Lord's Prayer</a>. The first question asks what is thine only comfort in life and in death, and answers that the believer belongs, body and soul, to Jesus Christ. Each statement rests on biblical texts. Lord's Day 30, in editions after the first, condemns the Catholic mass as accursed idolatry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> approved the catechism in 1563. Strict Lutherans kept attacking it, and in 1566 Frederick defended it at the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> against charges brought by Maximilian II of violating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a>, saying it rested on scripture. It then spread quickly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> synods at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesel">Wesel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordrecht">Dort</a> and the Hague adopted it, and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dort</a> made it one of the Three Forms of Unity with the Belgic Confession and the Canons of Dort. Ministers were to preach on a section each Sunday.</p><p>A 1719 Palatinate edition including Lord's Day 30 provoked so strong a Catholic reaction that Elector Charles III Philip banned it, until protests from Reformed countries reversed the ban. It influenced the Westminster Shorter Catechism, and many Reformed churches, especially of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> origin, still use it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heidelberg Catechism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick III created a church council to run the Reformed church and sponsored the <b>Heidelberg Catechism</b> of 1563, chiefly the work of Zacharias Ursinus, and in 1582 the territory received a common law code, the Landrecht.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>New texts gave the churches their shape: Luther’s German Bible and his Small Catechism of 1529, the Augsburg Confession drawn up by Philip Melanchthon in 1530, Thomas Cranmer’s Book of Common Prayer of 1549 and Reformed statements of faith such as the <b>Heidelberg Catechism</b> of 1563.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1559, when the senior electoral line died out, Duke Frederick III of Simmern became Elector Palatine and made the Palatinate Calvinist, issuing the <b>Heidelberg Catechism</b> in 1563.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-simmern">Palatinate-Simmern</a></li></ul></section>
